'''Lavington Common''' is a {{convert| 31.2 |ha|acre|abbr=off|adj=on}} biological [[Site of Special Scientific Interest]] south-west of [[Petworth]] in [[West Sussex]].<ref name=dsv>{{cite web|url= https://designatedsites.naturalengland.org.uk/SiteDetail.aspx?SiteCode=S1000528&SiteName=&countyCode=46&responsiblePerson=&SeaArea=&IFCAArea=  |title=Designated Sites View: Lavington Common | series= Sites of Special Scientific Interest|publisher=Natural England|accessdate = 1 May 2019}}</ref><ref name=map>{{cite web|url= https://magic.defra.gov.uk/MagicMap.aspx?startTopic=Designations&activelayer=sssiIndex&query=HYPERLINK%3D%271000528%27|title=Map of Lavington Common|series= Sites of Special Scientific Interest|publisher=Natural England|accessdate=  1 May 2019}}</ref> It is a [[National Trust]] property.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/lavington-common |title= Lavington Common |publisher=National Trust|accessdate=  1 May 2019}}</ref>

This site has wet and dry heath, acid grassland and woodland. It has a rich community of invertebrates, especially spiders. Common trees in the woods are [[silver birch]], [[downy birch]] and [[Quercus robur|oak]], while the shrub layer is dominated by [[Pteridium aquilinum|bracken]] and [[Rubus fruticosus|bramble]].<ref>{{cite web|url= https://designatedsites.naturalengland.org.uk/PDFsForWeb/Citation/1000528.pdf  |title=Lavington Common citation|series= Sites of Special Scientific Interest|publisher=Natural England|accessdate=  1 May 2019}}</ref>

